\description{
Performs Gene Set Enrichment Analysis (GSEA) using ranked gene lists
derived from differential expression results generated by \code{rna.compare()}.

Gene ranking can be based on moderated t-statistics or log2 fold-change.
The function integrates MSigDB gene sets via \pkg{msigdbr} and performs enrichment
using \pkg{fgsea}, supporting multiple organisms and gene ID types.

Annotation is performed automatically using
organism-specific \pkg{org.*.eg.db} databases.
}
\details{
This function operates on results generated by \code{rna.compare()},
ensuring consistency between differential expression and enrichment analysis.

The selected comparison defines the gene ranking used in GSEA.

For zebrafish, GO collections (C5) are not available in MSigDB and are
automatically replaced with Reactome pathways (C2:CP:REACTOME).
}
\section{Common gene set configurations}{


The MSigDB database is organized into collections (e.g., "C2", "C5"),
which may not be intuitive. Below are biologically meaningful configurations
for common analysis goals:

\strong{General biology (Hallmark pathways)}
\itemize{
\item Concise, non-redundant representation of major biological processes
\item Recommended as a starting point
\item \code{geneset_collection = "H"}
}

\strong{Classical pathways (Reactome, KEGG, BioCarta)}
\itemize{
\item Detailed signaling and metabolic pathways
\item More granular but also more redundant
\item \code{geneset_collection = "C2"}
\item \code{geneset_subcollection = "CP"}
}

\strong{Reactome pathways (highly curated)}
\itemize{
\item Structured, hierarchical pathway knowledgebase
\item Good balance between detail and interpretability
\item \code{geneset_collection = "C2"}
\item \code{geneset_subcollection = "CP:REACTOME"}
}

\strong{Gene Ontology (GO)}
\itemize{
\item Broad functional annotation (can be redundant)
\item \code{geneset_collection = "C5"}
\item Subcollections:
\itemize{
\item \code{"GO:BP"} – biological processes
\item \code{"GO:MF"} – molecular functions
\item \code{"GO:CC"} – cellular components
}
}

\strong{Cell type signatures}
\itemize{
\item Gene sets representing specific cell populations
\item Useful for inferring cellular composition or activation
\item \code{geneset_collection = "C8"}
}

\strong{Immunologic signatures (C7)}
\itemize{
\item Immunologic gene sets (stimuli, cell states, perturbations)
\item Particularly useful in infection, vaccination, and inflammation studies
\item \code{geneset_collection = "C7"}
}

\strong{Oncogenic signatures}
\itemize{
\item Gene sets derived from oncogenic pathway perturbations
\item Useful for cancer-related analyses
\item \code{geneset_collection = "C6"}
}

\strong{Tip: Choosing a collection:}
\itemize{
\item Use Hallmark ("H") for a clean global signal
\item Use C2/Reactome for pathway-level interpretation
\item Use GO (C5) for hypothesis generation
\item Use C7/C8 when biological context (immune/cell types) is central
}

\strong{Interpretation:}
Positive normalized enrichment scores (NES) indicate pathways enriched
in the test condition, while negative NES values indicate enrichment
in the reference condition.
}

\section{Gene ranking}{

\itemize{
\item \code{stat}: prioritizes genes with strong effect size and low variance.
\item \code{log2fc}: emphasizes magnitude of change.
}
}

\section{Visualization}{

\itemize{
\item Enrichment plots show running enrichment score for individual pathways.
\item Heatmaps summarize normalized enrichment scores (NES).
}
}

\section{Pipeline context}{

This function is typically used after \code{rna.normalize()} and before
downstream analyses such as \code{rna.compare()}, or clustering.
}

